      "confidence": 0.9,
      "curator_note": "Concrete: fluoroethane molecular ion CH3-CH2-F(+.) loses CH3. (the larger alkyl) to give CH2=F(+); this is the minor of the two competing channels (25%, dH = 1.3 eV). The C1-C2 bond alpha to the F radical site cleaves and a C2=F(+) pi bond forms. That the smaller H. loss (100%, eq 8.40a) dominates over this larger CH3. loss is the 'unusually large hydrogen-atom loss' that violates the loss-of-largest-alkyl rule (Section 8.2). Charge+radical are localized on F only to give the alpha-cleavage arrows a real origin (encoding convention B). Book draws 0 electron-pushing arcs: equation 8.40 is printed as a plain net-reaction line '-> CH3. + CH2=F(+) (25%)'. All three moves <1,2,3> are curator-supplied to balance the alpha C-C homolysis and are NOT drawn on the page: (1) the F(+.) odd electron into the forming C2=F pi bond, (2) one electron of the C1-C2 sigma bond into that same pi bond, (3) the second C1-C2 electron onto the departing methyl radical."
    },
